You've gotten lots of good answers.

Here's another answer:

   When you altar camp, nothing forces you to
   sacrifice _all_ the monster corpses. Eat as many
   as you like, sacrifice only when another one
   arrives right after you ate the last one. Don't
   eat any death-dropped food voluntarily, save that
   for the long dry spells when the RNG isn't giving
   any monsters, or the hallways are all blocked with
   slow moving monsters keeping the fast ones from
   reaching you.

   Many sacrifice effects ignore monster size, so eat
   the most nourishing ones and sacrifice the "trash".

   As an extreme cure for that latter "blocked
   hallways" problem, pickaxe out the entire level
   before you start. That way there are no
   bottlenecks, all monsters get created where you
   can see them, all monsters come straight to you.

   As a variation, leave one wall or line of stone
   at a distant part of the level, perpendicular to
   your line of sight, with several empty rows of
   grid cells behind it so no bottlenecks happen, and
   perhaps the game's "out of line of sight if
   possible" monster creation rules will find all of
   them coming to you from that one direction, giving
   you lots of time to decide how to handle them in
   the order they will arrive.

   Since the monsters can also see _you_ the length
   of their journey, it will help to have reflection
   or displacement or invisibility to somewhat
   protect you from zaps, breath attacks, and for the
   latter two, missile weapons.

   This whole "clear the level" plan, since it gives
   _you_ no bottlenecks to use to your advantage,
   works better if the altar is near a staircase so
   you can run for cover if too big a horde to handle
   come for you all at once.

   It can be a lot of fun to altar camp on a cleared
   out level. Engraving Elbereth so the arriving
   dwarves and rock moles can be left alive to clear
   the level _for_ you can be fun too. Last time I
   did the whole scheme, I also chose to name each
   monster as it appeared, just to give some added
   zest to the combat and other messages.

